// Annex G Table G.1, "Limiting exponential function": the OVI Verilog-A v1.0
// spelling `$limexp( expression )` was replaced in v2.0 by `limexp( expression
// )`, a syntax change. The retired form is not an alias -- a `$` name is a
// system function (§9), and §4.5.13 defines the analog operator under the bare
// name only. Accepting both is the backward-compatibility leak Table G.1
// exists to close.
//
// The current spelling is covered by ch04_expressions/25_limexp.va.
//
// NO CODE IS PINNED, and that is deliberate. The LRM's position is that the NAME
// does not exist: `$limexp` is absent from Table 9-11 and from A.8.2, so the
// conforming diagnosis is an unknown function, not "unsupported". E0801
// "unsupported system function" would be the wrong rule — it is VerA's
// capability class for ch9 functions it declines to implement (§9.13 $random,
// §9.16 $simprobe) — and it is also not the code this gap would close under:
// VerA aliases the spelling in codegen (`$limexp` beside `limexp`), and deleting
// that alias drops the call into codegen's abort path, not into a diagnostic at
// all. Naming a code VerA will never emit here is how an xfail outlives its gap:
// the XPASS trip-wire waits for a code that never comes. This module's only
// defect is the retired spelling, so `DiagnosticsReported` is exactly the claim
// "somebody diagnosed it".
//
//! lrm G.1
//! lrm 4.5.13
//! reject DiagnosticsReported
module annex_g_limexp_v1(p, n);
  inout p, n; electrical p, n;
  analog I(p,n) <+ $limexp(V(p,n));
endmodule
